# f1 = textOf. The slot of `badge` holds a reference of 32 bits, and the read gives it as it lies. Where proofs are checked at run time (testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime, which --aot-harden=proof-checks
# stands for) a value was asked only where it lay boxed, as a cell, as a number or as a truth value: one that lay as a narrow reference was asked by nobody. Now it is asked as it lies: the word is a
# reference to a cell, or a marker that the checked type has; else the program stops by name. Only then is it decoded and the cell looked at. As users build, nothing is asked.
# RUN(ASKED): walk f1 mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# ASKED: [[read]]: L0 @Cell32 = GetSlot {{*}}key="badge"
# ASKED-NEXT: = CheckProof [[read]] checkedType=L0 {{*}}source=Field
# RUN(UNASKED): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# UNASKED: @Cell32 = GetSlot {{*}}key="badge"
# UNASKED-NOT: source=Field
# RUN(HELD): emit f1:Body mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# HELD: Int32 @[[asked]] = AboveEqual(
# HELD-NEXT: = Branch(@[[asked]],{{*}}rare
# RUN(PLAIN): emit f1:Body mechanisms=all
# PLAIN-NOT: = AboveEqual(
# RUN(RIGHT): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# RIGHT: b2
# RIGHT-NEXT: exited with 0
